div0.entryFree:
sălārĭārĭus,
			ii, m.
		salarium, one who receives pay or salary, a salaried person, Dig. 17, 1, 10, § 9;
					Inscr. Orell. 3507;
			4074.

scriblīta (scribilīta), ae,
		f., a
				kind of tart, Cato, R. R. 78;
				Plaut. Poen. prol. 43;
					Petr. 35, 4; 66,
			3; Mart. 3, 17, 1.

scyphus, i, m., = σκύφος, a cup, goblet (cf.: calix, poculum), Plaut. As. 2, 4, 38 ; Varr. ap. Gell. 3, 14, 3; Cic. Verr. 2, 4, 14, § 32; Tib. 1, 10, 8; Verg. A. 8, 278; Hor. C. 1, 27, 1; id. Epod. 9, 33 al.; Plin. 34, 14, 40, § 141; 34, 17, 48, § 163: illuseras heri inter scyphos, i. e.
while drinking,
over our wine, Cic. Fam. 7, 22 init. : candelabri scyphi, Vulg. Exod. 37, 17 .

sĕcābĭlis,
		e, adj.
		seco, that may be cut (post-class.), Lact. de Ira Dei, 10, 8;
					id. Epit. 36, 5;
					Hier. in Isa. 5, 18, 4;
					Aus. Ecl. 1, 7.

sēmĭ-sŏnans,
			antis, adj.
		sono, halfsounding, semi-vocal: littera, i.
			e. a semivowel, App. de Mundo, 16, 29;
					Ter. Carm. Her. p. 2406 P.;
					Isid. 1, 4, 4.

Sĕrăphīn or Sĕrăphīm, plur. indecl., = ,
				the Seraphim, a higher order of angels
			among the Hebrews, Vulg. Isa. 6, 2;
					Prud. Cath. 4, 5;
					Isid. Orig. 7, 5, 24
		sq.

sēvĭrātus or sexvĭrātus,
			ūs, m.
		id., the dignity of a sexvir, the
			sexvirate, Petr. 71, 12;
					Inscr. Grut. 400, 7;
				150, 4.

signĭfex,
		fĭcis, m.
		signum-facio, an image-maker, carver,
				statuary (post-class.),
				App. M. 2, p. 116, 13;
					Arn. 6, 199;
					Mart. Cap. 1, § 36;
					Sid. Ep. 6, 12.

sī-quis or sī-qui, sīqua, sīquid or sīquod, or separately, sī quis, etc., indef. pron. (v. Zumpt, Gr. § 740), if any, if any one: si quis recte Tractaret, Ter. Heaut. 1, 1, 100 : si qui (filius) natus esset, Cic. Clu. 12, 33 : si qua tui Corydonis habet te cura, Verg. E. 7, 40 .—Adverb.: si qui and si qua, if in any way, if by any means: si qui, Liv. 3, 64 fin. ; Plaut. Trin. 1, 2, 83: si qua, id. Cist. 1, 3, 35 ; Verg. A. 1, 18; Hor. C. 3, 14, 19: si quo,
if any whither, Cic. Att. 8, 2, 1 ; also,
if for any purpose, Liv. 37, 28 : si quid,
if at all, Verg. A. 5, 688 ; cf. 2. quis.

sŏphĭa (i long,
				Prud. Sym. 122;
			id. Cath. 1613;
			Fortun. Suppl. 2, 62),
		ae, f., = σοφία, wisdom (pure Lat. sapientia): sophiam, sapientia quae
			perhibetur, Enn. ap. Fest. p. 325 Müll. (Ann.
			v. 227 Vahl.); Afran. ap. Gell. 13, 8, 3; Mart. 1,
				112, 1; cf. Sen. Ep. 89, 6;
				Lact. 3, 16, 10 (in Cic. Off.
				1, 43, 153, written as Greek).

spĕcĭālĭtas,
			ātis, f.
		specialis, particularity, peculiarity, Anton. Gramm. ap. Front. Diff. Verb. p. 2197 P.;
					Tert. adv. Marc. 5, 8;
					Isid. 2, 25, 6.

stĭbădĭum,
			ii, n., = στιβάδιον, a semicircular seat or couch, Plin. Ep. 5, 6, 36;
				Serv. Verg. A. 1, 698;
					Sid. Ep. 1, 11 med.; 2, 2
			med.; Mart. 14, 87
			in lemm.;
			Inscr. Orell. 2358.

strīga, ae, f.
		1. strix, a woman that brings harm to children, a hag, witch, Petr. 63;
			cf. Fest. s. v. strigem, p. 314 Müll.: striga, γυνὴ φαρμακίς, Gloss. Philox.

styptērĭa, ae,
			f., = στυπτηρία, alum, Dig. 27, 9, 3, § 6;
					Plin. Val. 1, 14 med.

suāvĭfĭco,
			ātus, sum, 1, v. sem. dep. [suavis-facio], to make acceptable or dear, Isid. 17, 8, 11;
					Arn. in Psa. 103.

suffĕrentĭa (subf-), ae, f.
		suffero, a bearing, enduring, toleration, sufferance
			(post-class.), Tert. adv. Marc. 4, 15;
					id. Or. 4 med.; Vulg. Jacob. 5, 11;
					id. Ecclus. 16, 14.

surio, īre, v. n.,
			to be in
			heat (cf. subare), Fest. s. v. suillum, p. 310 Müll.;
					App. Mag p. 298, 23;
					Arn. 5, 177.

synăloephē (-līphē, -loepha), ēs,
		f., = συναλοιφή,
		the contraction of
				two syllables into one, either by synæresis or by
			crasis; 
				in later gram. applied esp. to elision, =
					collisio, elisio,
				Quint. 9, 4, 109
			; Charis. p. 249 P.;
					Diom. p. 437 ib.;
					Mart. Cap. 3, § 267;
					Serv. Verg. A. 2, 16.
